    "q-105133": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105133",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=x+2?$\n\nI. $f(-1)\\ne f(1)$\n\nII. $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n\nIII. $f$ is an injection",
      "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268846/assets/3b849edf2ae8976e0abd5e0aa43180ad61d90b01d6f229c2b7fc7898ea860400.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=(-1)+2=1$ and $f(1)=1+2=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
