    "q-105218": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105218",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nII. $g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=(x+1)^{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={log}_{2}⁡{2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(-2),$ but $0\\ne -2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
